item(733,'P001425','problem',r"Evaluate Cauchy's determinant",r'Problem1 asks for \(\det(1/(x_i-y_j))\).',r'If \(x_i\ne y_j\) for every i,j, then \[\det_{1\le i,j\le n}\frac1{x_i-y_j}=\frac{\prod_{i<j}(x_j-x_i)(y_i-y_j)}{\prod_{i,j}(x_i-y_j)}.\] Repeated x or y values give zero, consistently with the formula.',r'This is the classical double alternant, checked in \citet[Section~2.1, equation~(2.7)]{krattenthaler1999determinant}; his1999 exposition is not the first publication. It recurs from item1981-541.3 in the earlier index.','Solved before the seminar. The difference-denominator sign is included explicitly.',refs=['krattenthaler1999determinant'],problem='GP-1987-24')
problem(24,'Cauchy double-alternant determinant',r'Evaluate \(\det(1/(x_i-y_j))\) under the nonvanishing denominator hypotheses.',['1981-541.3','1987-733.1'],'2026-11: classical solved identity; the factorization and sign convention are given in the occurrence entry.',['krattenthaler1999determinant'])
item(733,'P001425','problem','Real roots of the Rodrigues polynomial',r'Problem2 asks to prove that the nth derivative of \((x^2-1)^n\) has only real roots.',r'For every integer \(n\ge1\), \(\frac{d^n}{dx^n}(x^2-1)^n=2^nn!P_n(x)\), where \(P_n\) is the Legendre polynomial. It has exactly n simple roots, all in \((-1,1)\). Repeated Rolle arguments applied to the endpoint roots of multiplicity n already prove the required reality.',r'The Rodrigues formula and orthogonal-polynomial zero theorem are recorded in \citet[18.5(ii),18.2(vi)]{nist2026dlmf}; item1968-160.15 records the earlier seminar connection.','Solved classical exercise. n=0 gives a nonzero constant and no roots.',refs=['nist2026dlmf'],problem='GP-1987-25')
problem(25,'Real zeros in the Legendre Rodrigues formula',r'Prove that the nth derivative of \((x^2-1)^n\) has n real simple zeros in \((-1,1)\).',['1968-160.15','1987-733.2'],r'2026-11: classical, by Rolle\textquotesingle s theorem or orthogonality.',['nist2026dlmf'])
item(733,'P001425','problem','Discontinuities of a pointwise limit of continuous functions',r'Problem3 asks whether a pointwise limit on an interval can be discontinuous, have a dense set of discontinuities, or be discontinuous everywhere.',r'A real-valued pointwise limit of continuous functions on a nondegenerate real interval is Baire class1 and has a dense G-delta set of continuity points. It may be discontinuous (for example \(x^n\) on \([0,1]\)), and discontinuities may be dense (Thomae\textquotesingle s function, with value \(1/q\) at a reduced rational \(p/q\) and0 at irrationals). It cannot be discontinuous everywhere.',r'\citet[p.~173, theorem and reference to the1899 thesis]{baire1900nouvelle} provides the classical criterion for pointwise continuous approximation. The category conclusion applies to interval domains, which are Baire spaces.','All three questions are settled: yes, yes, no. Pointwise convergence must not be replaced by uniform convergence.',refs=['baire1900nouvelle'],problem='GP-1987-26')
problem(26,'Possible discontinuities of Baire-class-one functions',r'For a pointwise limit of real continuous functions on an interval, determine whether discontinuities can occur, be dense, or fill the interval.',['1987-733.3'],r'2026-11: yes to the first two; no to the last, by Baire\textquotesingle s theorem.',['baire1900nouvelle'])
item(733,'P001425','problem','Conformally map the radially slit disk',r'Problem4(a) draws a disk cut along a radius and asks for a conformal map to a disk.',r'For \(\Omega=\{z:|z|<1\}\setminus[0,1)\), choose \(w=\sqrt z\) with \(0<\arg z<2\pi\). Then w maps onto the upper half-disk; \(h=(1+w)/(1-w)\) maps that onto the first quadrant. Therefore \(f(z)=(h^2-i)/(h^2+i)\) is a biholomorphism from \(\Omega\) to the unit disk. A rotated slit is treated by an initial rotation.',r'The existence is a classical case of the mapping theorem originating in \citet[pp.~37--39 of the checked transcription]{riemann1851grundlagen}. The elementary displayed composition is an annotation of the exercise, not a formula found in the notebook.','Solved. The slit endpoint0 is removed, so no normalization f(0)=0 is imposed.',refs=['riemann1851grundlagen'],development='annotated',problem='GP-1987-27',reconstruction='The unit radius and positive-real slit choose coordinates for the picture; the construction is supplied here.')
item(733,'P001425','problem','Conformally map the dumbbell domain with a thin corridor',r'Problem4(b) draws two unit disks joined by a corridor of width \(10^{-6}\), marks A at a disk centre, and asks for a disk map sending A to0.',r'If the drawn union is an open connected simply connected proper plane domain \(\Omega\) and \(A\in\Omega\), the Riemann mapping theorem gives a biholomorphism \(f:\Omega\to\mathbb D\) with \(f(A)=0\); fixing \(f\prime(A)>0\) makes it unique. Corridor width alone does not determine \(\Omega\): its length, boundary shape and junctions are also needed for a particular numerical formula.',r'This is the same established mapping theorem as in \citet{riemann1851grundlagen}. Thin corridors can make numerical conformal parametrization poorly conditioned, but the notes give no quantitative estimate to verify.','Existence and normalized uniqueness are settled. A unique explicit numerical map cannot be extracted from the incomplete geometric dimensions in the sketch.',refs=['riemann1851grundlagen'],literal='correct with missing hypotheses',problem='GP-1987-27',reconstruction='The topological hypotheses and derivative normalization are stated explicitly; no unseen corridor length is invented.')
problem(27,'Normalized conformal maps of the slit and dumbbell domains',r'Construct a disk map for a radially slit disk, and establish a normalized disk map for a simply connected dumbbell domain with its marked interior point sent to0.',['1987-733.4','1987-733.5'],'2026-11: classical mapping theory settles existence; the slit case has an elementary composition. The picture alone does not specify a numerical dumbbell map.',['riemann1851grundlagen'])
save()

item(734,'P001426','definition','A G-structure is a principal reduction',r'The first definition takes a principal subbundle \(P\subset F(M)\) for \(G\subset GL(V)\), then says that G acts transitively on P.',r'For a smooth n-manifold M and closed Lie subgroup \(G\subset GL(n,\mathbb R)\), a G-structure is a principal G-subbundle of its frame bundle. The right G-action is free and transitive on each fibre \(P_x\), not on all of P when the base has more than one point.',r'This is the standard definition recalled in \citet[Section~5.1]{alekseevskymichor1995cartan}; that later paper explicitly reviews older theory. The earlier index has the same reduction in1980-493.1.','Established; the global transitivity sentence is a faithful mathematical error in the notebook.',refs=['alekseevskymichor1995cartan'],literal='false as written',reconstruction='The word each fibre repairs the source statement.')
item(734,'P001426','construction','Tensor fields of constant algebraic type define reductions',r'The examples include a metric, a tensor in one canonical form, an endomorphism with fixed Jordan form, an almost complex structure and a nondegenerate2-form.',r'If a smooth tensor field takes values in one \(GL(n)\)-orbit and its adapted frames form a smooth reduction, its stabilizer is the structure group. A positive metric yields \(O(n)\); \(J^2=-I\) in dimension2m yields \(GL(m,\mathbb C)\); a nondegenerate alternating2-form yields \(Sp(2m,\mathbb R)\). An almost symplectic form becomes symplectic only with the additional differential condition \(d\omega=0\). An endomorphism requires locally constant algebraic type for its adapted frames.',r'These are the elementary G-structure examples in the framework of \citet[Section~5.1]{alekseevskymichor1995cartan}; the note itself distinguishes almost symplectic from symplectic.','Established. A pointwise tensor classification does not imply differential integrability of the resulting structure.',refs=['alekseevskymichor1995cartan'],literal='correct with missing hypotheses',reconstruction='The orbit/reduction smoothness and even-dimensional requirements are made explicit.')
item(734,'P001426-P001427','theorem','Equivalent description by a solder form',r'The second definition gives a principal G-bundle with a V-valued1-form \(\theta\), equivariance, and kernel equal to the vertical tangent space. The equivalence is explained using coframes.',r'For a faithful inclusion \(G\subset GL(V)\), a principal G-bundle \(P\to M\) with \(\dim M=\dim V\) and a horizontal equivariant form \(\theta\) satisfying \(\ker\theta_p=\ker d\pi_p\) determines a coframe \(T_xM\to V\) at each p over x. Equivariance \(R_g^*\theta=g^{-1}\theta\) makes these coframes a G-reduction, and conversely the tautological solder form of a reduction has these properties.',r'\citet[Section~5.1]{alekseevskymichor1995cartan} recalls precisely this solder-form formulation. It was established G-structure theory before1988.',r'The equivalence is standard. The quotient used in the notebook must be \(T_pP/T_p^vP\), which is canonically \(T_xM\).',refs=['alekseevskymichor1995cartan'],literal='false as written',reconstruction=r'The quotient\textquotesingle s base space and the right-action convention are corrected.')
item(734,'P001427','construction','First jets of sections as horizontal complements',r'The first jet \(j_x^1s\) is identified with a right inverse of the projection derivative and with a horizontal subspace.',r'For \(s(x)=p\), its first jet is determined by \(d s_x:T_xM\to T_pP\) with \(d\pi_p\circ d s_x=I\). Its image H complements the vertical space. Conversely every such linear splitting is realized by a local section to first order. At fixed p these splittings form an affine space modeled on \(\operatorname{Hom}(T_xM,\mathfrak g)\), identified with \(\operatorname{Hom}(V,\mathfrak g)\) using p.',r'\citet[Section~6.1]{alekseevskymichor1995cartan} gives exactly the jet/horizontal-space identification, as recalled standard theory.','Established. This is the unrestricted first-jet bundle, before any torsion normalization defining a smaller prolongation.',refs=['alekseevskymichor1995cartan'])
item(734,'P001427-P001428','definition','A principal connection as an equivariant horizontal distribution',r'The space of connections is written as the G-equivariant sections of \(P\prime\to P\); horizontal lifts give parallel transport along curves.',r'A principal connection is a smooth G-equivariant assignment \(p\mapsto H_p\) with \(T_pP=H_p\oplus V_pP\). Equivalently it is a \(\mathfrak g\)-valued1-form \(\omega\) reproducing fundamental vertical fields and satisfying \(R_g^*\omega=\operatorname{Ad}(g^{-1})\omega\). The horizontal-lift ODE defines parallel transport along piecewise smooth paths in its existence interval; for principal connections it extends along compact parameter intervals by local trivializations.',r'The principal-connection framework is recalled by \citet[Sections~2.6 and3]{alekseevskymichor1995cartan}. It is background, not a new canonical connection selected by every G-structure.','Established. The lecture defines the space of connections; it does not prove that torsion can always be set to zero.',refs=['alekseevskymichor1995cartan'])
item(734,'P001428','definition','The torsion function on the space of horizontal complements',r'Using the vertical parallelism and \(\theta|_H:H\to V\), the notes define \(t(p\prime)(\xi,\eta)=d\theta((\theta|_H)^{-1}\xi,(\theta|_H)^{-1}\eta)\).',r'For every horizontal complement H at p, this formula defines \(t(H)\in\operatorname{Hom}(\Lambda^2V,V)\). For a selected connection, it is the coefficient function of the torsion2-form \(d\theta+\omega\wedge\theta\) evaluated on horizontal vectors. The alternating2-form, rather than an unrestricted bilinear map, is the natural target.',r'\citet[Section~6.1]{alekseevskymichor1995cartan} records the same torsion function and its role in prolongation.','Standard construction. No invariant zero-torsion complement is guaranteed without an algebraic condition on the intrinsic torsion.',refs=['alekseevskymichor1995cartan'])
item(734,'P001428','claim','Change of horizontal complement and the Spencer alternation',r'The final sentence asserts G-equivariance and \(G\prime=\operatorname{Hom}(V,\mathfrak g)\)-equivariance of torsion, without displaying the action on its target.',r'If \(H_A\) is obtained from H by adding the fundamental vertical vector corresponding to \(A(v)\), then with right-action convention \(t(H_A)=t(H)-\delta A\), where \(\delta A(v,w)=A(v)w-A(w)v\). Thus the class in \(\operatorname{Hom}(\Lambda^2V,V)/\delta\operatorname{Hom}(V,\mathfrak g)\) is independent of H. G acts linearly; the full space of changes of complement acts affinely by this alternation. The kernel of delta is the first algebraic prolongation.',r'\citet[Sections~6.1--6.2]{alekseevskymichor1995cartan} explicitly uses the Spencer map to normalize torsion and define prolongation.','Established after specifying the affine action and sign convention. The phrase equivariant alone does not mean torsion is invariant under all changes of connection.',refs=['alekseevskymichor1995cartan'],literal='correct with missing hypotheses',reconstruction='The omitted action and its sign are supplied by the right-principal-bundle convention.')
correction('P001426','principal reduction, transitivity','G acts transitively on P','G acts freely and transitively on every fibre P_x','Scan inspected: global transitivity is written. The principal-bundle definition and dimension of the base require a fibrewise assertion.')
correction('P001427','quotient below the coframe map',r'T_pM/T_p^vM',r'T_pP/T_p^vP','Scan inspected: M appears in the quotient; p is a point of P, and its vertical tangent is in T_pP. Both transcriptions follow the manuscript.')
correction('P001428','torsion equivariance','Hom(V,g)-equivariance with unspecified target action','The change-of-complement action on torsion is affine via the Spencer alternation','Scan inspected; Sections6.1--6.2 of Alekseevsky--Michor give the precise prolongation construction.','both','ambiguity')
save()

item(735,'P001429','remark','A reading suggestion on thermodynamics and quasilinear equations',r'Gelfand recommends his 1959 paper in Uspekhi, volume 14, issue 2, saying that three pages contain all thermodynamics.',r'The identifiable reference is the 1959 exposition Some problems in the theory of quasi-linear equations, pp.~87--158. The remark recommends its thermodynamic discussion; it does not state a new theorem or identify a three-page passage precisely enough to extract one.',r'\citet[publication record]{gelfand1959quasilinear} confirms the exact title, volume, pages and receipt on 17 November 1958. The full thermodynamic section was not read in this research.','The bibliographic identification is secure. The phrase all thermodynamics is an informal assessment, not an assertion of mathematical completeness.',refs=['gelfand1959quasilinear'],match='thematic only',verification='publisher metadata checked')
item(735,'P001429','question','Complete invariants of two planes in four dimensions',r'Gelfand asks what should be called the angle between two two-dimensional planes in four-dimensional Euclidean space.',r'For two linear two-planes \(U,W\) in a four-dimensional real inner-product space, choose orthonormal frame matrices \(Q_U,Q_W\). The singular values of \(Q_U^TQ_W\) are \(\cos\theta_1,\cos\theta_2\), where \(0\le\theta_1\le\theta_2\le\pi/2\). This ordered pair is a complete invariant under the full orthogonal group, and every pair in that range occurs.',r'The singular-value characterization was already explicit in \citet[Section~3, Theorem~1]{bjorckgolub1973angles}, published in 1973. The earlier index records related angle questions in 1964-46.2. The same question is restated as problem 3 of record 737.','Solved classical finite-dimensional classification. One scalar angle loses information in this dimension.',refs=['bjorckgolub1973angles'],problem='GP-1987-28',reconstruction='The word planes is interpreted as linear subspaces and the acting group as the full orthogonal group, consistently with the following problem sheet.')
item(735,'P001429','question','Complete invariants of two closed Hilbert subspaces',r'The second question asks for all invariants of a pair of subspaces in a Hilbert space.',r'For an ordered pair of closed subspaces M,N of a real or complex Hilbert space, the four intersections \(M\cap N\), \(M\cap N^\perp\), \(M^\perp\cap N\), \(M^\perp\cap N^\perp\) contribute their Hilbert dimensions. On the remaining generic part \(K\oplus K\), the projections have forms \(P=\left(\begin{smallmatrix}I&0\\0&0\end{smallmatrix}\right)\) and \(Q=\left(\begin{smallmatrix}C^2&CS\\CS&S^2\end{smallmatrix}\right)\), where \(0<C<I\) means that C and \(S=(I-C^2)^{1/2}\) are injective positive contractions. Their endpoints may still belong to the continuous spectrum. The unitary or orthogonal equivalence class of C, together with the four dimensions, is complete.',r'\citet[Theorems~1--3]{halmos1969subspaces} gives the two-projection canonical form and its completeness; the paper was received in April 1969. The statement is repeated and elaborated in record 737.','Solved for closed Hilbert subspaces. A mere set of spectral values is insufficient: spectral measure class and multiplicity are part of the invariant.',refs=['halmos1969subspaces'],literal='correct with missing hypotheses',problem='GP-1987-32',reconstruction='Closedness, the full isometry group and the endpoint-kernel convention are supplied; these are essential for a projection-based answer.')
save()

item(736,'P001429','construction','Formal Euclidean correlations from a local action',r'The speaker writes correlation functions as \(Z^{-1}\int A_1(x_1)\cdots A_n(x_n)e^{-S[\varphi]}\mathcal D\varphi\), with \(S=\int\sigma(\varphi,\partial\varphi)\,d^2x\).',r'This is a formal prescription for multilinear Euclidean correlation distributions. To make it a construction one must specify a measure or regularized limit, composite fields and coincident-point renormalization, and ensure \(0<Z<\infty\) when an ordinary normalization is used. The displayed continuum symbol \(\mathcal D\varphi\) alone does not provide these data.',r'The established operator/correlation formulation is described by \citet[Introduction and Section~2]{belavinpolyakovzamolodchikov1984infinite}; a rigorous Euclidean reconstruction framework predates it in \citet[abstract]{osterwalderschrader1975euclidean}.','Established formal language, not a general existence theorem for every local action. Specific rigorously defined models require additional analytic input.',refs=['belavinpolyakovzamolodchikov1984infinite','osterwalderschrader1975euclidean'],literal='correct with missing hypotheses',match='partial',reconstruction='Distributional interpretation and analytic existence conditions are supplied. The 1975 reconstruction source was checked at metadata/abstract level only.')
item(736,'P001429-P001430','method','Operator products and recursive determination of correlations',r'The fields form an algebra with \(A_i(x)A_j(y)=\sum_k c_{ij}^{\,k}(x,y)A_k(y)\). The notes say these coefficients and the one-point functions determine all correlations by repeated expansion; translation invariance makes the coefficients functions of \(x-y\).',r'If the chosen field system has convergent OPE expansions in correlation functions on nested domains, and these expansions satisfy associativity, the OPE coefficients together with vacuum one-point functions determine higher correlation functions there by iteration and elsewhere by any justified analytic continuation. Formal expansions alone do not imply convergence or independence of the sequence of pairings.',r'\citet[Introduction, equation~(1.6)]{belavinpolyakovzamolodchikov1984infinite} explicitly formulates the OPE with convergence in a finite domain and associativity. This is background from 1984, not a result originating in the 1988 lecture.','Established under the stated OPE hypotheses. The notebook suppresses precisely the analytic and associativity conditions needed for the proposed recursion.',refs=['belavinpolyakovzamolodchikov1984infinite'],literal='correct with missing hypotheses',reconstruction='The convergence domains and compatibility of repeated expansions are restored.')
item(736,'P001430','definition','Stress tensor and the translation Ward identity',r'A conserved symmetric field \(T^{\mu\nu}\) is required to generate the derivative of a field by a surrounding contour integral. The repeated formula contracts \(\varepsilon_{\mu\nu}\,dx^\lambda\).',r'A translation-generating stress tensor satisfies \(\partial_\mu T^{\mu\nu}=0\) away from other insertions, with contact terms at insertions. With the normalization of the charge fixed, \(\oint T^{\mu\nu}(x)A(y)\varepsilon_{\mu\lambda}\,dx^\lambda=\partial_{y^\nu}A(y)\) expresses its action on a local field. Conservation justifies contour deformations avoiding other insertions.',r'\citet[Section~2, equations~(2.1)--(2.10)]{belavinpolyakovzamolodchikov1984infinite} develops the stress-tensor Ward identity. The notation in the first notebook display has the correct free index; its repetition does not.','Standard Ward-identity construction. The literal repeated contraction is ill-formed, and conservation cannot be imposed without contact terms at coincident insertions.',refs=['belavinpolyakovzamolodchikov1984infinite'],literal='false as written',reconstruction='The lambda contraction and the contact-term qualification repair the displayed identity.')
item(736,'P001430','claim','Reflection positivity requires a full quadratic form',r'For the reflection \(\vartheta(x^1,x^2)=(-x^1,x^2)\), the matrix \(\langle A_i^+(\vartheta x)A_j(x)\rangle\) is required to be positive definite for every x.',r'The Euclidean positivity axiom is positive semidefiniteness of \(\langle(\Theta F)^*F\rangle\) for every finite linear combination F of smeared field products supported in one open time half-space. The reflected form can have null vectors, which are quotiented out in Hilbert-space reconstruction. Positivity of the displayed same-point matrix is only a restricted necessary test and does not by itself imply the full axiom.',r'The opened record for \citet[abstract]{osterwalderschrader1975euclidean} confirms the corrected Euclidean reconstruction theorem; full-text retrieval was blocked, so no claim is made to have read that proof. The full quadratic-form condition is the explicit modern reconstruction used here.','Established framework. Strict pointwise positivity is too strong in the presence of null fields and too weak as a substitute for reflection positivity on all field products.',refs=['osterwalderschrader1975euclidean'],literal='correct with missing hypotheses',match='partial',verification='publisher metadata checked',reconstruction='The half-space, smearing, arbitrary finite sums and semidefinite quotient are missing in the notes.')
item(736,'P001430','question','Construct substantive models of the field axioms',r'After the algebra, symmetry and positivity requirements the speaker asks for meaningful examples of all this.',r'A definite conformal version asks for a local two-dimensional theory with associative OPE, a stress tensor and a positive state space. The unitary Ising conformal theory supplies such a standard example, with central charge \(c=1/2\) and primary weights \((0,0),(1/2,1/2),(1/16,1/16)\) in its diagonal local sector. This is an example answering the request, not a classification of all models.',r'\citet[Section~6, equations~(6.11)--(6.18)]{belavinpolyakovzamolodchikov1984infinite} already develops degenerate theories and their correlation functions in 1984; the lecture is expository. The exact positivity completion of a continuum path integral is a separate analytic question.','The existence request had established nontrivial examples before the talk. The notebook does not state a definite universal classification problem.',refs=['belavinpolyakovzamolodchikov1984infinite'],literal='correct with missing hypotheses',match='partial',verification='read the result',reconstruction='The Ising model is supplied as one precise response to the broad request. Section 6 of the cited paper gives its conformal weights and closed operator products.',confidence='high')
item(736,'P001431','claim','Conservation and tracelessness give chiral stress components',r'With \(z=x^1+ix^2\), the notebook defines \(T=T^{zz}\), \(\bar T=T^{\bar z\bar z}\), sets \(T^{z\bar z}=0\), then asserts \(\partial_{\bar z}T=\partial_z\bar T=0\).',r'For a genuine contravariant symmetric tensor, \(T^{zz}=T^{11}-T^{22}+2iT^{12}\) and \(T^{z\bar z}=T^{11}+T^{22}\). Conservation and zero trace imply \(\partial_zT^{zz}=0\), so the displayed contravariant T has the opposite chirality. The conventional holomorphic component is the covariant \(T_{zz}=(T^{11}-T^{22}-2iT^{12})/4\), satisfying \(\partial_{\bar z}T_{zz}=0\). Rescaling this component gives the normalization used in the OPE.',r'The holomorphic stress-tensor construction is standard in \citet[Section~2, equations~(2.6)--(2.10)]{belavinpolyakovzamolodchikov1984infinite}. Direct tensor transformation and the two real conservation equations expose the notebook index inconsistency.',r'The standard chiral conclusion is correct after an explicit index/chirality correction. The separate equality \(T^{z\bar z}=T^\mu{}_\mu\) on P001433 is consistent with contravariant indices and \(ds^2=dz\,d\bar z\); it should not be independently changed by a factor of two.',refs=['belavinpolyakovzamolodchikov1984infinite'],literal='false as written',match='partial',development='annotated',reconstruction='This calculation distinguishes contravariant from covariant components rather than silently using the same symbol for both.')
item(736,'P001431','remark','Composite fields and the limits of Grassmann nilpotence',r'The example lists electron and photon composites, says that powers \(\psi^5,\psi^6\) vanish because the spinor has four components, and then warns that a pointwise \(A^2\) need not exist.',r'For four classical Grassmann generators at one point every homogeneous product of degree above four vanishes. This elementary exterior-algebra statement does not bound all quantum composites: derivatives give additional generators, and operator-valued distributions require a specified renormalized product. Even the product of two bosonic fields at the same point is not automatically defined.',r'The field-system/OPE formulation of \citet[Introduction, equation~(1.6)]{belavinpolyakovzamolodchikov1984infinite} includes derivatives and composite fields. The finite Grassmann calculation is an elementary reconstruction, not a general theorem about electron-field operators.','The warning about coincident products is sound. The nilpotence assertion needs the restricted finite Grassmann-algebra interpretation; it is not an identity for every quantum spinor composite.',refs=['belavinpolyakovzamolodchikov1984infinite'],literal='correct with missing hypotheses',match='thematic only',reconstruction='The distinction between finite Grassmann generators and quantum local fields supplies the necessary scope.')
item(736,'P001431','construction','Contour modes of the stress tensor',r'The modes are defined by \(\oint z^{n+1}T(z)A(w)\,dz/(2\pi i)\) on a contour around w; \(L_{-1}=\partial_w\) is then asserted.',r'In a local coordinate \(t=z-w\), define \(L_n^{(w)}A(w)=\operatorname{Res}_{z=w}(z-w)^{n+1}T(z)A(w)\). The simple-pole Ward identity gives \(L_{-1}^{(w)}A=\partial_wA\). The global modes about the origin use \(z^{n+1}\); they act on fields at w by coordinate-dependent combinations and must be distinguished from local descendant modes.',r'\citet[Section~2, equations~(2.18)--(2.21); Section~3]{belavinpolyakovzamolodchikov1984infinite} gives the contour generators and Laurent modes. The earlier occurrence 1982-573.17 records the same stress-tensor construction.','Established. The source formula is valid as a global charge expression, but the local highest-weight formulas later on require a coordinate centred at the insertion or an insertion at zero.',refs=['belavinpolyakovzamolodchikov1984infinite'],literal='correct with missing hypotheses',reconstruction='The local/global coordinate distinction is made explicit.')
item(736,'P001432','theorem','The singular stress-tensor operator product',r'The Laurent expansion is assumed to start at order \(-4\); the coefficients are stated to be \(T_{-4}=cI/2\), \(T_{-3}=0\), \(T_{-2}=2T\), \(T_{-1}=\partial T\).',r'For the normalized holomorphic stress tensor of an ordinary local CFT with a scalar central term and the conformal Ward identity, \[T(z)T(w)\sim\frac{c/2}{(z-w)^4}+\frac{2T(w)}{(z-w)^2}+\frac{\partial T(w)}{z-w}.\] The absence of a third-order pole and the scalar fourth-order coefficient use the stress-tensor transformation law and vacuum assumptions. Reflection positivity alone does not determine this OPE.',r'The equivalent infinitesimal transformation with the Schwarzian central term is given in \citet[Section~2, equations~(2.12), (2.19)--(2.21)]{belavinpolyakovzamolodchikov1984infinite}, before this lecture.','Standard exact singular OPE under the stated CFT assumptions; regular terms contain further composite fields.',refs=['belavinpolyakovzamolodchikov1984infinite'],literal='correct with missing hypotheses',reconstruction='The notebook attributes more to positivity than it alone supplies; the Ward/vacuum assumptions are restored.')
item(736,'P001432','theorem','The two commuting Virasoro algebras',r'The boxed formula is \([L_n,L_m]=(n-m)L_{n+m}+\frac c{12}(n^3-n)\delta_{n+m,0}\), and an antiholomorphic copy is described.',r'Taking residues in the stress OPE yields the displayed commutator, with the central term multiplying the identity. The antiholomorphic modes satisfy the analogous relation, and left and right modes commute in the local theory. Modulo its centre this is the Witt algebra; integration of the central term requires a central extension or projective representation of the diffeomorphism group, not simply the unextended group.',r'\citet[Section~2, equation~(2.21)]{belavinpolyakovzamolodchikov1984infinite} states precisely these relations. Prior occurrences include 1982-573.1 and 1985-657.1--4. No reducibility or singular-vector problem is posed in this passage, so the distinct earlier problem GP-1982-03 is not relabelled as a new problem here.',r'Established algebraic relation. The restriction to \(n=-1,0,1\) has zero central term and recovers the projective conformal subalgebra.',refs=['belavinpolyakovzamolodchikov1984infinite'],literal='correct with missing hypotheses',reconstruction='The identity operator in the central term and the group-extension qualification are explicit.')
item(736,'P001432-P001433','remark','An asserted universal smallest length',r'The speaker says that lengths below \(10^{-31}\) cm have no meaning, independently of theory, from relations among universal constants, and asks what space could mean with such a restriction.',r'No axioms defining meaningful distance, observable localization or an admissible geometry are stated. A dimensional combination of constants gives a candidate scale; it does not by itself prove a lower bound on all measured distances or a theorem about spaces. Consequently no precise universal theorem can be reconstructed from this assertion.',r'The conformal-field-theory source \citet{belavinpolyakovzamolodchikov1984infinite} identifies the surrounding lecture but provides no identified result establishing this assertion. The search and source check did not identify an exact mathematical theorem behind it.','The literal philosophical/physical claim is not a definite mathematical open problem. The scanned exponent is retained; no updated numerical constant is substituted for it.',refs=['belavinpolyakovzamolodchikov1984infinite'],cls='F',literal='not determinable',match='none found',verification='unresolved lead',confidence='high',reconstruction='No metric or observational definition is invented.')
item(736,'P001433','remark','An unspecified adelic determinant algorithm',r'The interjection says that some determinants are best computed by an adelic method.',r'The determinant family, coefficient domain, input size, arithmetic-cost model and algorithm are all unspecified. Therefore the comparative claim best cannot be converted into a definite complexity theorem or numerical-analysis problem.',r'The surrounding references to p-adic physics and the prior adelic lecture do not identify a particular determinant algorithm. Searches of the stated phrase and determinant/adelic terminology did not produce an exact attributable match.','Unidentified as a precise mathematical claim; neither an open-status judgment nor a claim of computational superiority is warranted.',cls='F',literal='not determinable',match='none found',verification='unresolved lead',confidence='medium')
item(736,'P001433','remark','A broad comparison of polygon and polyhedron theory',r'The notes contrast complete knowledge of two-dimensional polyhedra with supposed little progress on three- and four-dimensional ones in two thousand years.',r'The objects and questions are unspecified: convexity, combinatorial type, realizability, face enumeration and metric rigidity lead to different problems. The passage states no quantified theorem that can be assigned a precise present status.',r'No exact problem is recoverable from the broad historical comparison. It is kept as a source remark rather than attached to an unrelated later polytope theorem.','Not identifiable as a specific research problem. This is not evidence that finite-dimensional polytope theory has had no development.',cls='F',literal='not determinable',match='none found',verification='unresolved lead',confidence='high')
item(736,'P001433','method','Coordinate variation and the conformal Ward identity',r'Varying \(x^\mu\) by \(\varepsilon^\mu(x)\) gives a sum of field variations plus \(\int\partial_\mu\varepsilon_\nu\langle T^{\mu\nu}A_1\cdots A_n\rangle\). Integration by parts turns the variation into contours around insertions.',r'For separated insertions, a stress tensor satisfying the distributional Ward identity gives the stated contour action. If \(\partial_\mu\varepsilon_\nu+\partial_\nu\varepsilon_\mu=g(x)\delta_{\mu\nu}\) and the stress tensor is symmetric and traceless, the bulk contraction vanishes away from insertions. Boundary terms, contact terms and any anomalies must be handled in the chosen theory; the unregularized path integral is only a motivation.',r'\citet[Sections~2--3, especially equation~(3.4)]{belavinpolyakovzamolodchikov1984infinite} supplies the conformal Ward identities before the talk.','Established local derivation under the symmetry, regularity and boundary hypotheses. It does not establish conformal invariance for an arbitrary action.',refs=['belavinpolyakovzamolodchikov1984infinite'],literal='correct with missing hypotheses',reconstruction='The contact and boundary terms omitted by the formal integration by parts are stated.')
item(736,'P001434','construction','Primary fields, descendants and conformal families',r'Primaries satisfy \(L_n\phi=\bar L_n\phi=0\) for \(n>0\), \(L_0\phi=h\phi,\bar L_0\phi=\bar h\phi\), with \(h,\bar h\ge0\); the algebra is written as a direct sum of their descendant families.',r'At the origin a highest-weight state has the displayed annihilation and eigenvalue conditions. Applying negative modes generates its highest-weight module, with null states quotiented out in an irreducible unitary family. In a unitary positive-energy theory \(h,\bar h\ge0\). A direct sum of such families requires an appropriate discrete semisimple spectrum; it is not automatic for every conformal theory or every noncompact model.',r'\citet[Section~3, equations~(3.8)--(3.13)]{belavinpolyakovzamolodchikov1984infinite} presents primary fields and conformal families. The result predates this lecture.','Standard within the stated unitary highest-weight framework. The algebraic direct-sum sentence needs spectral/semisimplicity hypotheses, and the source does not pose a new singular-vector classification problem.',refs=['belavinpolyakovzamolodchikov1984infinite'],literal='correct with missing hypotheses',reconstruction='Coordinate origin, null-state quotient and the conditions for a direct-sum decomposition are supplied.')
item(736,'P001434','claim','Scaling weights, spin and the identity field',r'The identity has weights zero. Under \(z\mapsto\lambda z,\bar z\mapsto\bar\lambda z\), the notes assign the primary the factor \(\lambda^h\bar\lambda^{\bar h}\) and call \(h-\bar h\) its spin.',r'The second coordinate must be \(\bar z\mapsto\bar\lambda\bar z\). For a primary, \(h+\bar h\) is its scaling dimension and \(h-\bar h\) its spin. In a passive coordinate-change convention \(\phi_{\rm new}(\lambda z,\bar\lambda\bar z)=\lambda^{-h}\bar\lambda^{-\bar h}\phi(z,\bar z)\); active conventions reverse this placement. The identity has \((h,\bar h)=(0,0)\).',r'\citet[Introduction, equations~(1.16)--(1.17); Section~3]{belavinpolyakovzamolodchikov1984infinite} gives these transformation laws and weights.','Established after correcting the missing bar and specifying the active/passive convention. No sign correction of the weight factor is imposed without that convention.',refs=['belavinpolyakovzamolodchikov1984infinite'],literal='false as written',reconstruction='The missing coordinate conjugation is a literal error; the sign of the transformation factor is a convention that the source leaves unstated.')
correction('P001430','repeated translation Ward identity',r'\varepsilon_{\mu\nu}\,dx^\lambda',r'\varepsilon_{\mu\lambda}\,dx^\lambda','Scan inspected. The preceding displayed contour has the lambda contraction; the repetition leaves lambda uncontracted and repeats the free index nu.')
correction('P001431','complex stress components and equation (11)',r'T=T^{zz},\quad \partial_{\bar z}T=0','Use the covariant component T_{zz} for the holomorphic field, or exchange the asserted chiralities','Scan inspected: the indices are written upstairs. Direct tensor transformation for z=x^1+ix^2 gives partial_z T^{zz}=0. The trace identity on P001433 is consistent with upstairs indices and is not separately erroneous.')
correction('P001431','contour definition of descendant modes',r'z^{n+1} around insertion z_1',r'(z-z_1)^{n+1} for local descendant modes; z^{n+1} for global modes about the origin','The scan faithfully uses z^{n+1}; the distinction becomes material for the later highest-weight formulas.','both','ambiguity')
correction('P001434','barred scaling coordinate',r'\bar z\mapsto\bar\lambda z',r'\bar z\mapsto\bar\lambda\bar z','Scan inspected: the final z has no bar; both transcriptions preserve the missing conjugation.')
save()

item(737,'P001435','problem','An angle invariant for a line and a plane in three dimensions',r'The preamble calls an angle a complete orbit invariant and asks for its attainable values. Problem 1 asks for the angle between a line and a plane in three-dimensional space, explicitly separating intersecting and nonintersecting cases.',r'For an affine line \(\ell\) and an affine plane H under all Euclidean isometries, the direction angle \(\alpha\in[0,\pi/2]\), together with their distance \(\delta\ge0\), is complete, with the restriction \(\alpha>0\Rightarrow\delta=0\). When \(\alpha=0\), delta distinguishes a contained line from a disjoint parallel line. For linear subspaces only \(\delta=0\) occurs. Under origin-preserving orthogonal maps alone, additional positional data are needed for affine objects.',r'The directional angle is the elementary case of the singular-value characterization in \citet[Section~3, Theorem~1]{bjorckgolub1973angles}. The affine distance correction follows directly by moving the closest point or intersection to the origin.','Classical and solved after fixing the acting group. An angle alone is not a complete invariant of disjoint affine configurations.',refs=['bjorckgolub1973angles'],literal='correct with missing hypotheses',match='partial',reconstruction='Allowing translations is an explicit interpretation of the nonintersecting case, not an unannounced change from the preamble about orthogonal maps.')
item(737,'P001435','problem','An angle invariant for two planes in three dimensions',r'Problem 2 asks for the angle between two planes in three-dimensional space.',r'For linear two-planes U,W in \(\mathbb R^3\), their two principal angles are \(0,\theta\), with \(0\le\theta\le\pi/2\); theta is the acute angle between their normal lines and is a complete O(3) invariant. If affine planes are intended, distinct parallel planes additionally require their distance \(\delta>0\); nonparallel planes intersect, and their sole nonzero directional angle suffices under Euclidean isometries.',r'The linear statement is a dimension-three case of \citet[Section~3, Theorem~1]{bjorckgolub1973angles}. The affine supplement is elementary and is needed because the immediately preceding task includes disjoint affine objects.','Solved classical exercise. The source should not be read as claiming that one scalar classifies every origin-dependent affine placement.',refs=['bjorckgolub1973angles'],literal='correct with missing hypotheses',match='partial',reconstruction='The linear and affine interpretations are separated.')
item(737,'P001435','problem','Two principal angles classify two planes in four dimensions',r'Problem 3 repeats the question about two two-dimensional subspaces of a four-dimensional inner-product space, without choosing coordinate axes.',r'The complete O(4) invariant is \(0\le\theta_1\le\theta_2\le\pi/2\), with cosines the singular values of the cross-Gram matrix of orthonormal frames. Every such pair is realized by \(U=\operatorname{span}(e_1,e_2)\) and \(W=\operatorname{span}(\cos\theta_1e_1+\sin\theta_1e_3,\cos\theta_2e_2+\sin\theta_2e_4)\).',r'\citet[Section~3, Theorem~1]{bjorckgolub1973angles} gives the established singular-value formulation. This is the exact recurrence of 1987-735.2, with the range request supplied by the problem-sheet preamble.','Solved, including the full range of attainable invariants. The treatment for SO(4) requires the separate orientation discussion below.',refs=['bjorckgolub1973angles'],problem='GP-1987-28')
item(737,'P001435','problem','A vector and a subspace require magnitude as well as direction',r'Problem 4 gives \(v\in L\), a k-dimensional subspace V, and an inner product on the n-dimensional space L; it asks for their angle.',r'Under O(L), a pair (v,V) is classified by \(n,k,\|P_Vv\|,\|P_{V^\perp}v\|\). For \(v\ne0\), these are equivalently the norm of v and the angle \(\alpha\) defined by \(\cos\alpha=\|P_Vv\|/\|v\|\). Every pair of nonnegative component norms is possible when \(0<k<n\); if k=0 the first is zero, and if k=n the second is zero. The zero vector is a separate orbit with no direction angle.',r'The projection formula is the rank-one case of \citet[Section~3]{bjorckgolub1973angles}; completeness follows by independently rotating the two orthogonal components.','Solved. The problem-sheet definition of a complete invariant makes normalization material: angle alone does not distinguish vectors of different lengths.',refs=['bjorckgolub1973angles'],literal='correct with missing hypotheses',match='partial',reconstruction='The source does not require a unit vector; its length is therefore retained.')
item(737,'P001436','problem','Finite-dimensional pairs: complete invariants and their exact range',r'Problem 5 asks for the angle between subspaces V,W of dimensions k,m in an n-dimensional inner-product space.',r'Let \(r=\min(k,m)\) and \(q=\max(0,k+m-n)\). The ordered principal angles \(0\le\theta_1\le\cdots\le\theta_r\le\pi/2\), with at least q zero entries, classify ordered pairs under O(n), or under U(n) in the complex version. Every list satisfying exactly these restrictions is attainable. The dimensions of the ambient space and of both subspaces are also part of the classification when they are not fixed.',r'The cross-Gram SVD in \citet[Section~3, Theorem~1]{bjorckgolub1973angles} gives the principal angles. Orthogonal principal two-planes plus intersection and unmatched directions give both completeness and the stated dimension restriction.','Solved classical finite-dimensional problem, including attainability. Replacing the list by its minimum, maximum or average angle discards information.',refs=['bjorckgolub1973angles'],problem='GP-1987-28',reconstruction='The exact dimension-forced zero-angle condition supplies the range requested in the preamble.')
item(737,'P001436','problem','One, two and three subspaces under the general linear group',r'Problem 6 removes the inner product and asks for complete invariants of one subspace, a pair and an ordered triple, with GL(n) as the acting group.',r'For one subspace the dimension suffices. For a pair, the dimensions of U,V and \(U\cap V\), together with n, suffice. Over \(\mathbb R\) or \(\mathbb C\), an ordered triple decomposes uniquely into eight one-dimensional membership types indexed by subsets of \(\{1,2,3\}\), and copies of the two-dimensional configuration of three distinct lines \(\operatorname{span}e_1,\operatorname{span}e_2,\operatorname{span}(e_1+e_2)\). The nine multiplicities are complete invariants. All nonnegative multiplicities occur, subject to the ambient dimension \(\sum_Sa_S+2b=n\) and any prescribed subspace dimensions.',r'The introduction of \citet[Section~1]{gelfandponomarev1971quadruples} separates the easy r=2,3 cases from the four-subspace problem. The exact nine-type classification already appears in the prior index at 1972-277.5--6; it is established finite-type linear algebra, not a new 1988 conjecture.','Solved, with a discrete finite list of building-block types and arbitrary admissible multiplicities. Pairwise intersection dimensions alone do not capture the two-dimensional triple block.',refs=['gelfandponomarev1971quadruples'],problem='GP-1987-35',reconstruction='The source omits the base field. The stated elementary decomposition is for real or complex vector spaces; its precise multiplicities answer the completeness and range requests.')
item(737,'P001436','problem','Two lines in the Minkowski plane',r'Problem 7 fixes the quadratic form \(-x_1^2+x_2^2\) and asks for complete invariants of two lines under its preserving group.',r'For coincident lines the causal type (negative, positive, null) is complete. For two distinct unoriented lines choose nonzero spanning vectors u,v and form \(G=\left(\begin{smallmatrix}\langle u,u\rangle&\langle u,v\rangle\\\langle u,v\rangle&\langle v,v\rangle\end{smallmatrix}\right)\). Its equivalence class under \(G\mapsto DGD\), with invertible real diagonal D, is a complete O(1,1) invariant. The attainable matrices are precisely the symmetric two-by-two matrices of signature (1,1), equivalently determinant less than zero. This formulation includes null lines without division by their zero norms.',r'This two-dimensional answer follows directly by sending the basis (u,v) to another basis with the same Gram matrix. It is a simple case of the broader form-preserving classification later treated by \citet[Introduction and Theorem~1]{sergeichuk1990pairs}; the elementary case was already available before the talk.','Solved. An ordinary cosine alone cannot cover spacelike, timelike and null cases.',refs=['sergeichuk1990pairs'],problem='GP-1987-31',reconstruction='The diagonal rescaling identifies lines rather than chosen vectors; no orientation or time-orientation constraint is imposed.')
item(737,'P001436','problem','Pairs in finite-dimensional indefinite and degenerate forms',r'Problem 8 extends the pair problem to higher Minkowski dimensions, arbitrary indefinite signature, and degenerate forms such as \(x_1^2+x_2^2\) on \(\mathbb R^3\).',r'Fix a finite-dimensional vector space over a field of characteristic different from two with a symmetric, skew-symmetric or Hermitian form F, allowing degeneracy, and two labelled subspaces. Classify triples \((V_F;U_1,U_2)\) up to form-preserving linear isomorphisms carrying each labelled subspace to its counterpart. Sergeichuk gives a complete canonical decomposition for this problem, up to the classification of the Hermitian forms over the relevant fields; for the real symmetric setting these residual forms are determined by their inertia. Isometry blocks, their multiplicities and their allowed field parameters are the complete data, rather than only ordinary principal-angle numbers.',r'\citet[Theorem~1]{sergeichuk1990pairs} supplies a precise later classification covering the finite-dimensional indefinite and degenerate formulations. The paper was received on 13 September 1988 and published in 1990; its introduction mentions a partial earlier announcement, so this date is not asserted as the first discovery of every component.','The finite-dimensional problem has a canonical-form solution. The opened result does not settle the subsequent infinite-dimensional indefinite version, and no such extrapolation is made.',refs=['sergeichuk1990pairs'],cls='B',problem='GP-1987-31',reconstruction='The field, form type, labelled nature and isometry category are fixed explicitly. The notebook asks only the real symmetric cases; the cited theorem treats a broader family.')
item(737,'P001436','problem','Passing from the full orthogonal group to the special group',r'After the indefinite variants the sheet asks that all listed problems be considered for both O(n) and SO(n).',r'For a nondegenerate real quadratic space, an O-orbit splits into two SO-orbits exactly when the stabilizer has no determinant-minus-one element; this follows because SO has index two. In the Euclidean ordered-pair case, it splits exactly when all four intersections \(U\cap W,U\cap W^\perp,U^\perp\cap W,U^\perp\cap W^\perp\) vanish. Equivalently, \(n=2k=2m\) and every principal angle lies strictly between zero and \(\pi/2\). Otherwise a reflection in one of those intersections keeps the orbit unsplit. For a degenerate form the full preserving group can have arbitrary nonzero determinant on its radical, so this index-two argument cannot simply be transferred.',r'The criterion is an elementary index-two orbit calculation, with the Euclidean stabilizer read from the principal-angle blocks of \citet[Section~3]{bjorckgolub1973angles}. The invariant is augmented by a binary orientation choice exactly in the split case.','Solved for the stated nondegenerate groups. The notation O(n),SO(n) in the degenerate extension needs a definition and cannot be used to infer an index-two subgroup there.',refs=['bjorckgolub1973angles'],literal='correct with missing hypotheses',match='partial',development='annotated',reconstruction='The stabilizer criterion and the degeneracy caveat are supplied; the latter prevents an incorrect universal two-orbit claim.')
item(737,'P001436','problem','Four subspaces under GL: the Gelfand--Ponomarev classification',r'Problem 9 asks for complete invariants of a quadruple of subspaces and explicitly calls it the content of the work of Gelfand and Ponomarev.',r'For an ordered quadruple in a finite-dimensional vector space over an algebraically closed field of characteristic zero, decomposition into indecomposable quadruples reduces the classification to the discrete families and one-parameter families listed by Gelfand--Ponomarev. With defect \(d=\sum_i\dim U_i-2\dim V\), indecomposable defects are \(-2,-1,0,1,2\); the continuous regular families have defect zero. Their block multiplicities and parameters give complete invariants. Real versions require real canonical blocks rather than silently assuming every eigenvalue is real.',r'\citet[Section~2, Assertions~1--2; Section~3, Theorem~1]{gelfandponomarev1971quadruples} gives the explicit list and decomposition. It was received on 21 October 1970 and published in 1971. Earlier occurrences are 1970-198.1--13 and 1972-277.7.','Solved before the talk. The source explicitly attributes existing work; it is not an open classification request in the historical sense.',refs=['gelfandponomarev1971quadruples'],literal='correct with missing hypotheses',problem='GP-1987-36',reconstruction='The algebraically closed characteristic-zero hypothesis is the one actually read in the original paper. The labelled order of the four subspaces is retained.')
item(737,'P001436','problem','Principal angles of the two tensor subspaces',r'Problem 10, attributed to A. A. Kirillov junior, gives an inner-product space L, tensors \(a,b\in L\otimes L\), and asks for the angle between \(a\otimes L\) and \(L\otimes b\) in \(L^{\otimes3}\). The preamble explicitly asks which invariant values are attainable.',r'For a finite-dimensional real or complex Hilbert space of dimension d and nonzero tensors a,b, write their coefficient matrices as A,B in one orthonormal basis. The d principal cosines are the singular values of \((\overline A B)^T/(\|A\|_F\|B\|_F)\). If d is at least two, the complete range is all ordered \(s_1\ge\cdots\ge s_d\ge0\) with \(\sum_js_j\le1\). If d=1 the only cosine is 1. Zero tensors change the subspace dimensions and are excluded from this d-angle statement.',r'The singular-value interpretation is an application of the established principal-angle theorem of \citet[Section~3, Theorem~1]{bjorckgolub1973angles}. The chapter annotation at Section~\ref{dev:tensor-angles} proves the normalized cross-Gram formula and the exact range directly. It is an explicit solution of this finite-dimensional exercise, with no claim of novelty.', 'Solved in the stated finite-dimensional reconstruction, including attainable values. The source only says inner-product space; finite dimension is inferred from its placement before the separate Hilbert-space section, and is recorded rather than hidden.',refs=['bjorckgolub1973angles'],literal='correct with missing hypotheses',match='partial',development='annotated',problem='GP-1987-29',reconstruction='Finite dimension, nonzero tensors and normalization are explicit additions. The real source case is covered; the complex Hilbert variant is also stated. The complete range proof is the chapter annotation, not a result claimed to be in the 1973 paper.')
item(737,'P001437','problem','Hilbert pairs, discrete angles and continuous spectral data',r'Problem 11 asks for a complete invariant of two Hilbert subspaces and for examples with a countable set or a continuum of angle values.',r'For closed M,N, use the four intersection dimensions and the generic positive contraction C from 1987-735.3. On a separable Hilbert space its spectral measure class together with multiplicity gives the full continuous invariant. For discrete examples take \(C=\operatorname{diag}(\cos\theta_j)\) with \(0<\theta_j<\pi/2\); for a continuum take \(K=L^2(0,1)\), \(Cf(t)=tf(t)\), and \(N=\{(Cf,Sf):f\in K\}\) in \(K\oplus K\). The latter has continuous spectrum [0,1] for C but no eigenvectors at those spectral values.',r'\citet[Theorems~1--3]{halmos1969subspaces} proves the canonical form and completeness. Its 1969 chronology makes this an established answer when the sheet was written.','Solved for closed Hilbert subspaces. A countable or continuous set of numbers without spectral type and multiplicity is not a complete orbit invariant.',refs=['halmos1969subspaces'],literal='correct with missing hypotheses',problem='GP-1987-32',reconstruction='The projection formulation requires closed subspaces. Separability is imposed only for the familiar measure-and-multiplicity formulation and the displayed examples.')
item(737,'P001437','problem','The indefinite Hilbert-space version is not specified',r'Problem 12 asks the same question for a Hilbert space with indefinite metric.',r'To formulate a definite classification one must specify, for example, a bounded nondegenerate fundamental symmetry J, the topology of the subspaces, whether they are regular for the indefinite form, and the equivalence group of bounded J-unitaries. A degenerate or unbounded form leads to different categories. These choices are absent, and the finite-dimensional canonical-block theorem does not answer all of them.',r'\citet{halmos1969subspaces} treats positive Hilbert geometry; \citet[Introduction and Theorem~1]{sergeichuk1990pairs} treats finite-dimensional form spaces. Neither identifies a single exact infinite-dimensional assertion from the unspecified notebook wording.','No definite remaining open problem can be assigned from this statement alone. This entry is unidentifiable in category, not a declaration that all Krein-space pair classifications are unknown.',refs=['halmos1969subspaces','sergeichuk1990pairs'],cls='F',literal='not determinable',match='thematic only',verification='unresolved lead',confidence='high',reconstruction='The possible hypotheses illustrate why the source is insufficient; none is selected and attributed to the author.')
item(737,'P001437','problem','Realize a prescribed invariant relative to a fixed subspace',r'The first comment emphasizes the inverse task: given a subspace and an angle invariant, construct another subspace having that invariant.',r'For a fixed k-plane U in \(\mathbb R^n\), any m-plane principal-angle list satisfying 1987-737.5 is realized by rotating orthogonal principal blocks, using the forced zero-angle intersections to fit the ambient dimension. For a fixed closed Hilbert subspace M, the Halmos data are realizable exactly when the required orthogonal summands fit: \(\dim M=d_{11}+d_{10}+\dim K\) and \(\dim M^\perp=d_{01}+d_{00}+\dim K\). On the generic summands set \(N=\{(Cx,(I-C^2)^{1/2}x):x\in K\}\), and add the chosen intersection summands.',r'The construction is the inverse of the canonical forms in \citet[Section~3]{bjorckgolub1973angles} and \citet[Theorem~2]{halmos1969subspaces}. Both were established before the seminar.','Solved for the finite positive and closed Hilbert settings just specified. The cardinal-dimension restrictions are necessary, even when an abstract angle operator has already been chosen.',refs=['bjorckgolub1973angles','halmos1969subspaces'],literal='correct with missing hypotheses',problem='GP-1987-28',reconstruction='The finite and Hilbert versions are made explicit; no realization claim is made for the undefined indefinite category.')
item(737,'P001437','construction','Encode operator similarity by four subspaces',r'The final comment says that a linear operator can be specified by a quadruple of subspaces.',r'For \(T:E\to E\), the ordered quadruple \(E\oplus0,\ 0\oplus E,\ \{(x,x)\},\ \{(x,Tx)\}\) in \(E\oplus E\) determines T up to similarity. Any isomorphism preserving the first two subspaces is block diagonal; preservation of the diagonal forces the two blocks to coincide, and preservation of the last graph is exactly the similarity relation. Conversely any similarity gives such a configuration isomorphism.',r'\citet[Section~1, Example~1]{gelfandponomarev1971quadruples} explicitly gives the graph construction, with the final two labels interchanged. It is already indexed as 1970-198.3.','Established exact reduction. The source is an expository reminder rather than a new representation theorem.',refs=['gelfandponomarev1971quadruples'],problem='GP-1987-36')
problem(28,'Principal-angle classification and realization in finite dimension',r'For two subspaces of fixed dimensions k,m in an n-dimensional positive inner-product space, determine a complete orthogonal or unitary orbit invariant and all its attainable values, including realization relative to a fixed first subspace.',['1964-46.2','1987-735.2','1987-737.3','1987-737.5','1987-737.14'],'2026-11: solved classical problem. The ordered principal angles lie in [0,pi/2], with at least max(0,k+m-n) zeros; these are exactly the restrictions.',['bjorckgolub1973angles'])
problem(29,'Angles and attainable invariants of tensor subspaces',r'For nonzero \(a,b\in L\otimes L\) with finite-dimensional positive inner-product L, classify the pair \(a\otimes L,L\otimes b\subset L^{\otimes3}\) by principal angles and determine their full possible range.',['1987-737.11'],r'2026-11: the chapter annotation at Section~\ref{dev:tensor-angles} proves that for \(\dim L=d\ge2\), precisely the nonnegative ordered principal cosines of sum at most one occur; for d=1 only cosine one occurs. The result is presented as a worked solution of an established linear-algebra exercise, without a novelty claim.',['bjorckgolub1973angles'])
problem(31,'Pairs of subspaces in finite-dimensional indefinite or degenerate forms',r'Fix a finite-dimensional real vector space with a symmetric bilinear form, possibly indefinite or degenerate. Classify ordered pairs of subspaces under the full group preserving that form, including null subspaces.',['1987-737.7','1987-737.8'],'2026-11: finite-dimensional canonical forms are available in Sergeichuk (1990). This does not transfer to the unspecified infinite-dimensional indefinite variant.',['sergeichuk1990pairs'])
problem(32,'Complete invariants and realization for closed Hilbert-subspace pairs',r'Classify an ordered pair of closed Hilbert subspaces under ambient isometries, retaining both discrete intersections and the generic spectral invariant; determine the admissible data and give discrete and continuous examples.',['1987-735.3','1987-737.12','1987-737.14'],'2026-11: solved by the two-projection canonical form (Halmos, 1969). The invariant includes spectral measure class and multiplicity, not just the spectrum as a set; dimension constraints govern realization.',['halmos1969subspaces'])
problem(35,'Configurations of at most three linear subspaces',r'Classify one subspace, ordered pairs and ordered triples of subspaces of a finite-dimensional real or complex vector space under GL, and characterize the attainable invariant data.',['1972-277.5','1972-277.6','1987-737.6'],'2026-11: solved finite-type classification; triples have eight one-dimensional membership blocks and one two-dimensional three-line block, with nine nonnegative multiplicities.',['gelfandponomarev1971quadruples'])
problem(36,'Four-subspace classification and operator encoding',r'Classify ordered quadruples in a finite-dimensional vector space up to simultaneous linear isomorphism, and include the similarity problem for one operator through its graph configuration.',['1970-198.1','1970-198.3','1970-198.6','1970-198.13','1972-277.7','1987-737.10','1987-737.15'],'2026-11: the algebraically closed characteristic-zero problem was classified by Gelfand--Ponomarev in 1971 using explicit indecomposable families. It is an established result explicitly recalled in this problem sheet.',['gelfandponomarev1971quadruples'])
correction('P001435','school problems and acting group','Nonintersecting line/plane pairs after a definition using orthogonal maps','Specify affine Euclidean isometries for the disjoint cases, or retain additional origin-dependent position data','Scans P001435--P001436 inspected. Linear subspaces always meet at zero; disjoint affine pairs need at least a distance invariant.','both','ambiguity')
correction('P001436','O(n),SO(n) after the degenerate-form variants','Use the two group names for all variants without definition','Specify the preserving group for the degenerate form; determinant-one need not have index two','Scan inspected. Arbitrary invertible maps on the radical preserve a degenerate form and can have any nonzero determinant.','both','ambiguity')
save()
item(738,'P001438','definition','Local polynomial functionals and the variational derivative',r'The operator is printed as \(L=\partial^N+\sum_{i=0}^{N-1}u_{N-1}\partial^i\); functionals are integrals of polynomials in coefficient jets. Equation (3) defines the variational derivative by first variation.',r'Fix a differential algebra \(\mathcal A\) of differential polynomials in \(a_0,\ldots,a_{N-1}\), with total derivative \(D\), and write \(L=D^N+\sum_{j=0}^{N-1}a_jD^j\). A formal local functional is a class \(\int P\in\mathcal A/D\mathcal A\). Its Euler derivative is \(\delta F/\delta a_j=\sum_{k\ge0}(-D)^k\,\partial P/\partial a_j^{(k)}\). For smooth periodic coefficients this agrees with integration by parts in \(\delta F=\int\sum_j(\delta F/\delta a_j)\delta a_j\).',r'This formal calculus is established in \citet[Section~1]{gelfanddikii1978jets} and \citet[Section~2]{gelfanddorfman1979hamiltonian}; both precede the seminar. The repeated coefficient subscript in (1) is incompatible with the later independent variations and Miura formula.','Standard variational calculus. Local functionals form a vector-space quotient; multiplication of densities does not descend to multiplication of their quotient classes.',refs=['gelfanddikii1978jets','gelfanddorfman1979hamiltonian'],literal='false as written',reconstruction=r'The independent coefficients are renamed \(a_j\) to avoid silently mixing the source conventions \(u_j\) and \(u_{N-j}\). Periodicity or the formal quotient removes boundary terms.')
item(738,'P001438','claim','Disjoint additivity does not imply a smooth local density from continuity alone',r'Gelfand says that increments supported in two disjoint places add independently, and that this property together with continuity forces a finite-jet integral of the displayed type.',r'On an open subset \(U\subset C^\infty(M)\), the additivity condition is \(F(\phi+\psi+\chi)=F(\phi+\psi)+F(\psi+\chi)-F(\psi)\) when \(\operatorname{supp}\phi\cap\operatorname{supp}\chi=\varnothing\) and all arguments belong to \(U\). Continuity alone is insufficient: \(F(u)=u(x_0)\) is continuous and additive, but its differential is the Dirac distribution, not integration against a smooth density. A precise characterization additionally assumes Bastiani smoothness, each \(DF_u\) a compactly supported smooth density, and smooth dependence of this density on \(u\) in the test-function topology. The conclusion is a finite-jet integral in a neighborhood of each configuration, with order allowed to depend on that neighborhood.',r'\citet[Theorems~I.2 and VI.3]{brouderetal2018functionals}, first circulated 3 May 2017 and published in 2018, proves this characterization with the stated regularity hypotheses. Its locally finite-order conclusion is weaker than a single global bound on jet order.','The literal continuity-only claim is false. A precise regular version is proved; no general continuity theorem or global finite-order theorem is inferred from it.',refs=['brouderetal2018functionals'],cls='B',literal='false as written',match='partial',problem='GP-1987-33',development='annotated',speaker='I. M. Gelfand',reconstruction='The function space, topology, smooth density requirement and locally bounded jet order are reconstructed from the later theorem; they are not present in the notebook.')
item(738,'P001439','construction','Length and area as local geometric functionals',r'Length of a curve and area of a surface are listed as local examples before eigenvalue functionals are called transcendental.',r'For a smooth immersed curve in a Riemannian manifold, length is \(\int |\dot\gamma(t)|\,dt\). For a smooth immersed surface, area is the integral of the square root of the determinant of the induced metric. These densities depend on first jets and are smooth on the open set of immersions. Disjointly supported perturbations therefore have additive increments, provided the perturbations remain in that open set.',r'These are classical examples of the local variational calculus formalized in \citet[Section~1]{gelfanddikii1978jets}; their smooth, rather than polynomial, jet dependence is the geometric extension explicitly allowed in \citet[Section~2, footnote]{gelfanddorfman1979hamiltonian}.','Established local examples. Smoothness at a degenerate map is not implied: the immersion hypothesis matters for the square-root densities.',refs=['gelfanddikii1978jets','gelfanddorfman1979hamiltonian'],literal='correct with missing hypotheses',match='thematic only',speaker='I. M. Gelfand',reconstruction='The target metric and nondegeneracy of the immersion are supplied.')
item(738,'P001439','question','A hierarchy of transcendence defined by variational differentiation',r'Type zero means local; type one means that \(\delta F/\delta u\) is local; higher types are recursive. The first eigenvalue is a motivation, and the speaker predicts functionals of all types in characteristic-class theory.',r'The notes do not specify how a function-valued differential, a distributional one-form or a higher variation is to belong to the same class as a scalar functional. Nor do they specify the operator and boundary conditions defining \(\lambda_1\), the treatment of spectral multiplicities, equivalence modulo constants, or the global configuration space. The recursive words therefore do not determine a single classification problem or a theorem that the hierarchy is strict at every level.',r'The named antecedent is identifiable as \citet[Introduction and Section~1]{gabrielovgelfandlosik1976atiyah}, which studies characteristic-class boundary functionals. That concrete paper does not in the sections read formulate the unrestricted recursive hierarchy asserted here; later local-functional characterizations likewise do not settle an unspecified notion of transcendence.','The general hierarchy remains unidentifiable as a definite theorem from this source. This F classification concerns the missing definition, not an assertion that the named characteristic-class work was never developed.',refs=['gabrielovgelfandlosik1976atiyah','brouderetal2018functionals'],cls='F',literal='not determinable',match='thematic only',verification='unresolved lead',confidence='medium',speaker='I. M. Gelfand',reconstruction='No modern differential-cohomology or spectral-invariant theory is silently substituted for the proposed hierarchy.')
item(738,'P001439','remark','The identified Atiyah--Patodi--Singer functional antecedent',r'Gelfand recalls work with Gabrielov and an uncertainly written Losik concerning characteristic classes and functionals whose variations have a local character.',r'The identifiable setting is a compact Riemannian manifold with piecewise smooth boundary and a top-degree characteristic form of the tangent bundle. One seeks face terms such that the characteristic integral plus boundary-face integrals depends on the intrinsic boundary metric and topological data. The referenced paper constructs differential forms on faces and a generalized de Rham cocycle formulation. This is a specific geometric problem, distinct from an unrestricted classification of all scalar functionals by successive variations.',r'\citet[Introduction and Section~1, Definitions~1.1--1.12]{gabrielovgelfandlosik1976atiyah}, received 22 December 1975 and published in 1976, is the exact author/topic match. Its introduction explicitly separates the proposed boundary-functional structure from what is known in general.','The historical reference is securely identified and predates the talk. Only the introduction and the foundational definitions were read, so no general boundary decomposition beyond their stated scope is certified.',refs=['gabrielovgelfandlosik1976atiyah'],literal='correct with missing hypotheses',match='partial',speaker='I. M. Gelfand',reconstruction='The surname is identified bibliographically as M. V. Losik; the precise geometric setting is supplied by the opened primary paper.')
item(738,'P001439-P001440','problem','Variational calculus for sums and products of local functionals',r'Gelfand observes that local functionals are not an algebra, asks for calculus on sums and products, and writes a double integral involving jets at two points. He rejects treating the issue as only a formal adjunction of products.',r'On a specified smooth field space, the algebra generated by local scalar functionals consists of finite sums of finite products. Its differential obeys \(D(FG)=G\,DF+F\,DG\); higher derivatives obey the iterated Leibniz rule and have kernels on products of the base. Products are generally nonlocal: for \(F(u)=\int u\), the mixed second derivative of \(F^2\) in disjointly supported directions \(h,k\) is \(2(\int h)(\int k)\), which need not vanish. A general smooth double-integral kernel need not be a finite sum of separated kernels, so the displayed two-point expression can describe a broader class.',r'The elementary product calculus predates the talk. A precise later framework for smooth local and multilocal functionals, including locality criteria on products of manifolds, is given in \citet[Sections~II and VII.B--C]{brouderetal2018functionals}, circulated in 2017 and published in 2018. It supplies a useful rigorous setting without identifying a unique geometric axiom that the notebook demands.','The stated finite-product differentiation problem is settled; a chosen topology and a distinction between finite products and general multilocal kernels remain essential. The broader request to change geometry is not treated as one unresolved conjecture.',refs=['brouderetal2018functionals'],cls='B',literal='correct with missing hypotheses',match='partial',problem='GP-1987-37',speaker='I. M. Gelfand',reconstruction='Finite products, arbitrary two-point kernels, and the unspecified philosophical demand for new geometry are distinguished explicitly.')
item(738,'P001440','method','Hamiltonian formalism without multiplication of local functionals',r'The proposed way out invokes structures introduced by Gelfand and Dorfman, followed by connections, de Rham cohomology and Yang--Baxter brackets.',r'One precise construction starts with a Lie algebra \(\mathfrak g\) acting on a module \(M\), with no multiplication assumed on \(M\). Its Chevalley--Eilenberg forms and differential give a Cartan calculus. A Hamiltonian operator \(H\) from suitable one-forms to \(\mathfrak g\), with Lie-closed image and closed induced skew form, defines \(\{f,g\}=(Hdf)g\), satisfying Jacobi. In the variational example, \(M=\mathcal A/D\mathcal A\) and \(\mathfrak g\) consists of evolutionary derivations.',r'\citet[Section~1, Theorems~1.1 and 1.3; Section~2, Theorem~2.1]{gelfanddorfman1979hamiltonian}, received 16 April 1979, states and proves precisely this module-based construction. The subsequent bare references to connections and Yang--Baxter equations do not specify additional theorems to identify.','Established framework from 1979, directly relevant to the failure of a density quotient to be an algebra. No nonexistent multiplication on the quotient is needed.',refs=['gelfanddorfman1979hamiltonian'],match='partial',speaker='I. M. Gelfand',reconstruction='The exact Lie-algebra/module construction is identified; the later programme words are not converted into unsupported claims.')
item(738,'P001440','construction','Formal pseudodifferential symbols and composition',r'Equations (4)--(8) introduce symbol composition, a Laurent series bounded above in powers of \(\xi\), its differential part and its residue. The series is misleadingly labelled \(C(z)[[\xi]]\) and its summation indices disagree.',r'Over a characteristic-zero differential coefficient algebra, formal pseudodifferential symbols have the form \(A=\sum_{i\le N}a_i(z)\xi^i\), with arbitrarily negative but finitely many positive powers. Composition is \(A\circ B=\sum_{k\ge0}(\partial_\xi^k A)(\partial_z^k B)/k!\); every resulting coefficient is a finite sum. Put \(A_+=\sum_{i\ge0}a_i\xi^i\) and \(\operatorname{res}A=a_{-1}\). The inverse symbol \(D^{-1}\) is formal and is not a chosen analytic integration operator with boundary conditions.',r'This symbolic calculus is already used in \citet[Sections~2--5]{gelfanddikii1976fractional}; the original 1976 article and its composition convention were opened. It is foundational background for both Hamiltonian brackets.',r'Established formal algebra. The appropriate Laurent notation is \(\mathcal A((\xi^{-1}))\), with the specified noncommutative composition, rather than the ordinary power-series ring in positive powers of \(\xi\).',refs=['gelfanddikii1976fractional'],literal='false as written',reconstruction='The coefficient algebra, upper boundedness, matching dummy index and distinction between formal and analytic inverse derivatives are supplied.')
item(738,'P001440-P001441','construction','Residue trace and the pseudodifferential variational gradient',r'The gradient is \(\sum_{j=0}^{N-1}\xi^{-j-1}\circ\delta f/\delta u_j\), followed by \(\delta f=\oint\operatorname{res}(\delta L\circ\nabla f)\).',r'With \(a_j\) the coefficient of \(D^j\), set \(\operatorname{Tr}A=\int\operatorname{res}A\), modulo total derivatives or on the circle, and \(X_F=\sum_{j=0}^{N-1}D^{-j-1}\circ(\delta F/\delta a_j)\). Integration by parts gives \(\operatorname{Tr}[A,B]=0\) and \(\delta F=\operatorname{Tr}(\delta L X_F)\). The trace pairing determines the cotangent representative modulo the annihilator of variations of the monic differential operator.',r'The residue-symbol and variational calculus occurs before the talk in \citet{gelfanddikii1976fractional,gelfanddikii1978jets}. The formula is also a direct coefficient check using cyclicity of the integrated residue; no analytic trace-class assertion is involved.',r'Exact in formal or periodic variational calculus. The source gradient uses coefficient order \(u_jD^j\), whereas the later Miura line uses \(u_{N-j}D^j\), so the reindexing must be explicit.',refs=['gelfanddikii1976fractional','gelfanddikii1978jets'],literal='correct with missing hypotheses',reconstruction='A single coefficient convention and the formal trace quotient are fixed; the residue trace is not confused with the operator trace on a Hilbert space.')
save()
item(738,'P001441','theorem','The first Gelfand--Dikii bracket',r'Equations (11) and (13) assert that \(\{F,G\}_1=\operatorname{Tr}([L,X_F]X_G)\) gives a Lie bracket on the stated functionals.',r'For monic scalar differential operators of fixed positive order over the differential-polynomial algebra, use the residue gradient of item 1987-738.9. The map \(J_{1,L}(X)=[L,X]_+\) is Hamiltonian, and the displayed pairing is a skew bracket satisfying Jacobi on local functionals. The negative part of \([L,X_F]\) does not contribute when paired with the negative-order representative \(X_G\). The leading coefficient is fixed; the coefficient of \(D^{N-1}\) is constant along these Hamiltonian vector fields.',r'This is established before the talk. \citet[Section~5, Examples~2--3]{kupershmidtwilson1981modifying} states the exact formula and explains its restriction to zero subleading coefficient; \citet[Sections~10--11]{gelfanddikii1976fractional} supplies an earlier formal variational Lie-bracket construction.','The Jacobi assertion is proved in the specified formal setting; it is not a new open question in this report. Finite products of actual scalar functionals can be included by the Leibniz rule.',refs=['kupershmidtwilson1981modifying','gelfanddikii1976fractional'],literal='correct with missing hypotheses',reconstruction='The fixed-order space, quotient by total derivatives and harmless projection under the trace are made explicit.')
item(738,'P001441','theorem','The second bracket, compatibility, and the need for reduction',r'Equation (12) gives \(J_{2,L}(X)=L(XL)_+-(LX)_+L\), and equation (13) asserts the Lie property. The subsequent examples put the subleading coefficient equal to zero.',r'The pairing \(\{F,G\}_2=\operatorname{Tr}(J_{2,L}(X_F)X_G)\) is Poisson on monic scalar differential operators with all lower coefficients variable. It is compatible with the first bracket: every constant linear combination is Poisson. To pass to \(a_{N-1}=0\), the cotangent representative must be corrected by a \(D^{-N}\) term so that \(\operatorname{res}[X,L]=0\); the resulting Hamiltonian vector field is tangent to the constraint. Merely inserting \(a_{N-1}=0\) in the unreduced formula is insufficient.',r'\citet[Section~5, Examples~4--6; Theorems~8.6 and 11.2]{kupershmidtwilson1981modifying} gives the bracket with the opposite overall sign, proves the Hamiltonian property and explains the reduction. Its bibliography identifies the 1978 Gelfand--Dikii IPM preprint136 mentioned in the notebook. \citet[Sections~1 and 6]{khovanova1986gelfand} uses precisely the notebook sign.','Established before March1988. Overall sign changes preserve the Poisson property, but an omitted reduction changes the actual bracket and the Virasoro normalization.',refs=['kupershmidtwilson1981modifying','khovanova1986gelfand'],literal='correct with missing hypotheses',reconstruction='Compatibility and the tangency correction are supplied from the proved formulation. The source sign is retained, and its missing reduction is not concealed.')
item(738,'P001441','claim','The order-two example is Virasoro, but the printed mode constants disagree',r'For \(L=D^2+u\), the notes set \(F_n=\frac12(2\pi i)^{-1}\oint u(z)z^{n+1}dz\) and assert \(\{F_n,F_m\}=(n-m)F_{n+m}+(n^3-n)\delta_{n+m,0}/12\).',r'Fix \(\operatorname{Tr}=(2\pi i)^{-1}\oint\operatorname{res}\) and exactly the notebook pairing \(\{F,G\}=\operatorname{Tr}((J_2X_F)X_G)\). After reduction, the coefficient operator is \(J_u(f)=-\frac12D^3f-2uDf-(Du)f\). For the displayed \(F_n\), integration by parts gives \[\{F_n,F_m\}=\frac{m-n}{2}F_{n+m}-\frac{n^3-n}{8}\delta_{n+m,0}.\] Reversing the pairing reverses both signs. Suitable rescalings recover a Virasoro bracket, but they cannot be omitted while retaining the stated modes and claiming the printed constants.',r'\citet[Section~7, Lemma~6 and its preceding formulas]{khovanova1986gelfand} explicitly gives the reduced order-two operator and its coadjoint Virasoro interpretation, published in1986. The mode calculation above follows directly from that operator and was independently checked during this task.','The structural Virasoro identification is established. Under the explicit fixed convention the source mode formula is false; its numerical constants are not certified by an appeal to the structural identification alone.',refs=['khovanova1986gelfand','kupershmidtwilson1981modifying'],literal='false as written',match='partial',development='annotated',reconstruction='The normalized residue, reduction and order of the pairing are all stated so that the correction is reproducible. The ordinary Fourier/Laurent identity is used, not a quantum normal-ordering correction.')
item(738,'P001441','claim','The order-three bracket and the primary spin-three field',r'For \(L=D^3+u_2D+u_3\), the notes give a Virasoro bracket for \(u_2\), a mixed coefficient \(2n-m\), and an incomplete \(u_3,u_3\) formula containing a convolution quadratic in \(u_2\).',r'The reduced order-three second Gelfand--Dikii bracket is the classical \(W_3\) Poisson algebra. For the raw operator coefficients, the spin-three primary is \(w_3=u_3-\frac12Du_2\). Indeed, infinitesimal coordinate covariance gives \(\delta_f u_2=fDu_2+2(Df)u_2+2D^3f\) and \(\delta_f u_3=fDu_3+3(Df)u_3+u_2D^2f+D^4f\), so \(\delta_f w_3=fDw_3+3(Df)w_3\). The pure spin-three mixed mode formula belongs to this corrected field. The self-bracket has quadratic stress-tensor terms, but the notebook ellipsis is not a complete set of defining relations.',r'The Virasoro inclusion is treated in \citet[Section~9]{khovanova1986gelfand}. \citet[Sections~1 and 3]{lukyanov1988quantization} relates these classical brackets to their quantum \(W\)-algebra construction. The displayed primary change follows directly from the two coordinate-variation equations and clarifies the raw-coefficient ambiguity.',r'The classical algebra was known before the talk. Its identification does not validate the omitted terms or the numerical central coefficients of the notebook. A pure primary mixed bracket for the unchanged raw \(u_3\) is incorrect.',refs=['khovanova1986gelfand','lukyanov1988quantization'],literal='false as written',match='partial',reconstruction='The reduction and primary field change are supplied explicitly. No exact coefficient for the unfinished quadratic convolution is invented.')
item(738,'P001442','theorem','The classical Miura map is Poisson',r'The product \(L=(D+v_1)\cdots(D+v_N)\) defines the coefficient fields, and the modes of the \(v_i\) have independent Heisenberg brackets \(h\,n\delta_{ij}\delta_{n+m,0}\).',r'Take the \(v_i\) as independent differential variables with the constant diagonal first-order Hamiltonian structure. Expanding the ordered product makes every coefficient of \(L\) a differential polynomial in them. The induced bracket on those coefficients is the second Gelfand--Dikii bracket, with the common overall scale and sign fixed by the chosen pairing. Reduction to zero subleading coefficient requires the corresponding constraint \(\sum_i v_i=0\) and the reduced current bracket; independent currents cannot simply retain their unreduced brackets after that constraint.',r'\citet[Theorem~11.5, p.~436; Theorem~8.6 for the reduced case]{kupershmidtwilson1981modifying} proves the constant-current realization. The original cover gives1981, whereas current Springer metadata gives1980; receipt was February1980 and revision18August1980. \citet[equation~(1.5)]{lukyanov1988quantization} recalls the classical theorem before quantizing it.','Established Poisson realization. It is a nonlinear differential-polynomial map, not a globally invertible change of coefficient functions on every analytic function space.',refs=['kupershmidtwilson1981modifying','lukyanov1988quantization'],literal='correct with missing hypotheses',reconstruction='Formal differential variables, factor order, normalization and the separate constrained-current reduction are specified.')
item(738,'P001442','method','Known first-order factors give a solution by quadratures',r'Gelfand observes that \((D+v_1)(D+v_2)y=f\) can be solved by quadratures and compares differential Galois theory with solving algebraic equations by radicals.',r'If continuous \(v_1,v_2,f\) are known on an interval, put \(w=(D+v_2)y\) and \(V_i(x)=\int_{x_0}^xv_i(t)\,dt\). Then \(w=e^{-V_1}(C_1+\int_{x_0}^x e^{V_1(t)}f(t)\,dt)\) and \(y=e^{-V_2}(C_2+\int_{x_0}^xe^{V_2(t)}w(t)\,dt)\). These formulas solve the factored equation with two initial constants. The assertion presupposes known factors; it does not supply them over a prescribed differential field or impose global periodic boundary conditions.',r'This is the classical integrating-factor calculation. The factorization and differential-field viewpoint are also explicit in \citet[Section~9]{kupershmidtwilson1981modifying}. The brief mention of Picard--Vessiot theory is an analogy, not a complete solvability criterion stated in the source.','Correct under the stated coefficient and factorization assumptions. Factoring a general differential operator can be the difficult step, and quadratures need not lie in the original coefficient field.',refs=['kupershmidtwilson1981modifying'],literal='correct with missing hypotheses',match='thematic only',speaker='I. M. Gelfand',reconstruction='The two elementary integrating-factor formulas give the precise content of the remark without attributing a general differential-Galois theorem to it.')
item(738,'P001442','claim','Nonlinear Poisson relations do not destroy the Lie property',r'The notes say that a Lie algebra has become isomorphic to a quadratic algebra that is not a Lie algebra; Khovanova is named, and an unspecific comparison with Dynkin follows.',r'A Poisson algebra is always a Lie algebra under its bracket, even when brackets of selected generators are quadratic polynomials rather than linear combinations of those generators. Thus the span of the chosen coefficient fields may fail to be Lie-closed, while the full Poisson algebra still satisfies Jacobi. The Miura map is a Poisson realization, and the existence of a nonlinear formula does not make it an isomorphism onto the entire current algebra.',r'\citet[Section~1, Lemma~1; Section~7]{khovanova1986gelfand} explicitly constructs Gelfand--Dikii Lie algebras, directly contradicting the literal denial of a Lie structure. \citet[Theorem~11.5]{kupershmidtwilson1981modifying} states the Miura restriction theorem, not a universal isomorphism. The Dynkin remark names no definite algebra or theorem.','The literal formulation is false. The meaningful distinction is between a linearly closed generating space and nonlinear Poisson relations in the larger algebra.',refs=['khovanova1986gelfand','kupershmidtwilson1981modifying'],literal='false as written',match='partial',reconstruction='The meanings of generator span, Poisson algebra and realization are separated; no specific Dynkin theorem is guessed.')
save()
item(738,'P001442-P001443','construction','Quantum Miura coefficients from Heisenberg currents',r'The factors are normal ordered, \(L(z)=:(D+v_1(z))\cdots(D+v_N(z)):\), and the question is which relations the coefficient fields satisfy.',r'Work with formal radially ordered Heisenberg currents whose singular products are \(v_i(z)v_j(w)\sim h\delta_{ij}(z-w)^{-2}\). Choose the usual mode normal ordering and expand \(L=D^N+\sum_{j=1}^N W_jD^{N-j}\). This defines coefficient fields as normally ordered differential polynomials. The problem is to compute their singular operator products and prove closure, rather than to multiply arbitrary unregularized operator-valued distributions at a point.',r'\citet[Section~1, equations~(1.1)--(1.5)]{lukyanov1988quantization} makes precisely this construction. The paper was received25February1988, before the21March report, and published later in1988; it acknowledges Radul. The theorem belongs to Lukyanov, whom Radul explicitly reports.','The construction is established. The subsequent items identify the regularity equation and closure theorem answering the stated relation problem.',refs=['lukyanov1988quantization'],cls='B',literal='correct with missing hypotheses',problem='GP-1987-34',reconstruction='The formal mode algebra, radial order and normal-ordering convention supply the operator meaning suppressed by the notation.')
item(738,'P001443','theorem','Lukyanov conjugated regularity relation',r'With \(\sigma f(z,w)=f(w,z)\), the notes set \(\tau=\frac{\nu}{2}\log(z-w)^2\,\sigma\), \(\nu(\nu-1)=h\), and assert \(e^{-\tau}L(z)L(w)e^\tau[f]=O(1)\) for every holomorphic test function. The final symbol is marked uncertain between O and zero.',r'In the formal differential-operator algebra, define \(\sigma f(z,w)=f(w,z)\) and \(\tau=\frac{\nu}{2}\log((z-w)^2)\sigma\). Let \(f\) be holomorphic on the product of punctured coordinate planes, including the diagonal. For the quantum Miura operator and \(\nu(\nu-1)=h\), the conjugated expression has no singular part as \(z\to w\): \(e^{-\tau}L(z)L(w)e^\tau[f]=O(1)\). The logarithm is used locally with a compatible branch in the formal calculation. The conclusion is regularity, not that the expression vanishes identically.',r'\citet[Section~2.1, Proposition~2 and equations~(2.11)--(2.17)]{lukyanov1988quantization} is an exact match, including the parameter relation and the conjugation formula (2.15). The published formula resolves the notebook ambiguity in favour of \(O(1)\).','Proved in the original1988 paper. This exact primary match removes the need to classify the formula as an unidentified exchange relation.',refs=['lukyanov1988quantization'],cls='B',literal='correct with missing hypotheses',problem='GP-1987-34',reconstruction='The formal differential-operator action and regularity meaning are supplied from the original paper. The published formula identifies the intended O(1), without claiming the handwriting alone is unambiguous.')
item(738,'P001443','theorem','Quadratic closure and explicit hypergeometric coefficients',r'Equation (24) gives a sum of singular powers with coefficient products, but the bracket signs are ambiguous; the constants are said to involve hypergeometric functions.',r'The quantum Miura coefficient fields satisfy quadratic singular-product relations of the form \[W_i(z)W_j(w)+\sum_{k=2}^{i+j}\frac{\sum_{p+q=i+j-k}C_{ij}^{pq}(k)\,W_p(z)W_q(w)}{(z-w)^k}=O(1),\] with \(W_0=1\) and numerical coefficients depending on the quantum parameter. Their explicit formulas are obtained from the auxiliary coefficients expressed through generalized hypergeometric functions. This is a statement about ordered operator products and their singular parts; blindly interpreting every square bracket in the notebook as a commutator does not give the published theorem.',r'\citet[Theorem~1, equation~(1.6), and equations~(2.18)--(2.21)]{lukyanov1988quantization} proves this closure and gives the coefficient prescription. Theorem2 gives the associated reduced \(W_N\) construction after the current constraint is imposed with its appropriate covariance.','The definite relation problem is solved in the identified1988 work. The source formula records the correct quadratic structure, but its ambiguous bracket notation is not a fully reliable literal presentation.',refs=['lukyanov1988quantization'],cls='B',literal='correct with missing hypotheses',match='partial',problem='GP-1987-34',reconstruction='The ordered-product interpretation and finite singular sum are supplied from the publication; the unverified handwritten sign choices are not silently certified.')
item(738,'P001443','remark','A schematic quantum inverse-scattering exchange analogy',r'The final sentence compares the regularity formula with the quantum inverse-scattering method and writes \(L(z)L(w)=R^{-1}L(w)L(z)R\), without defining \(R\).',r'No auxiliary space, operator \(R\), spectral-parameter dependence, tensor-leg convention or equality modulo regular terms is specified. The written identity therefore does not determine a separate Yang--Baxter or exchange theorem. In particular it cannot replace the already identified conjugated regularity relation by an exact unrestricted operator equality.',r'\citet[Section~2.1, after equation~(2.6)]{lukyanov1988quantization} explicitly remarks on an analogy with a classical r-matrix, while its proved statement is the regularity relation identified above. No precise additional R-matrix theorem matching the final bare formula was established by the source check.','Unidentifiable as a distinct exact theorem; retained as an analogy. The surrounding quantum closure result is known and is not assigned open status because this final shorthand lacks a definition.',refs=['lukyanov1988quantization'],cls='F',literal='not determinable',match='thematic only',verification='unresolved lead',confidence='medium')
problem(33,'An intrinsic regularity criterion for local functionals',r'Characterize when a scalar functional on an open subset of \(C^\infty(M)\) is, locally in configuration space, an integral of a smooth finite-jet density; determine what must supplement disjoint-support additivity.',['1987-738.2'],r'2026-11: continuity and additivity alone are insufficient, as point evaluation shows. Under Bastiani smoothness, smooth-density differential and smooth dependence of the gradient, the exact local finite-jet characterization is proved by Brouder et al. (preprint2017; publication2018).',['brouderetal2018functionals'])
problem(34,'Relations among quantum Miura coefficients',r'For independent Heisenberg currents and \(L=:(D+v_1)\cdots(D+v_N):\), determine the singular operator products of the coefficient fields and prove closure of the resulting quantum Gelfand--Dikii algebra.',['1987-738.17','1987-738.18','1987-738.19'],r'2026-11: solved in Lukyanov (1988), received25February1988 before the Radul report. Theorem1 gives quadratic closure, Section2 its regularity relation and explicit coefficients, and Theorem2 the reduced \(W_N\) construction.',['lukyanov1988quantization'])
problem(37,'Variational calculus for products and multilocal functionals',r'Extend variational differentiation from local scalar functionals to finite sums and products, while distinguishing that generated algebra from arbitrary smooth kernels depending on jets at several base points.',['1987-738.6'],r'2026-11: finite products satisfy the ordinary iterated Leibniz calculus. Rigorous smooth-functional and multilocal settings are developed, including Brouder et al. (2017/2018). A topology and a specified class of kernels are needed for questions about completion; the notebook states no unique such completion problem.',['brouderetal2018functionals'])
